  "body": "Whether `a.pathname`, `.search` and `a.hash` should be URI decoded isn't specified AFAICT, and browsers vary widely.\n\nGiven this:\n\n``` JS\na = document.createElement('a');\na.href = \"/ö/o?ö/o#ö/o\";\nconsole.log(a.pathname, a.search, a.hash);\n```\n\nwe get:\n\n| UA | `.pathname` | `.search` | `.hash` |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Chrome | `/%C3%B6/o` | `?%C3%B6/o` | `#ö/o` ! |\n| Firefox | `/%C3%B6/o` | `?%C3%B6/o` | `#%C3%B6/o` |\n| Safari | `/%C3%B6/o` | `?%C3%B6/o` | `#%C3%B6/o` |\n| Edge | `/ö/o` | `?ö/o` | `#ö/o` |\n| IE11 | `ö/o` !! | `?ö/o` | `#ö/o` |\n\nI don't have a Windows machine at hand, the last two results were communicated to me over chat... Apparently, all but IE11 follow the living standard, but in a way that requires per getter feature detection:\n\n``` JS\n// Imagine we're in an event handler, and given `a` as defined above:\nvar hash = ((/ö/.test(a.hash) ? String : decodeURI)(event.currentTarget.hash);\n```\n\nOther URI getters are probably also concerned, I didn't test anything beyond these three.\n\nAlso, I don't know how trailing slashes are handled in MS Browsers. In the first three, they are preserved.\n\nIt would be nice if it could be harmonized, or at least if within UA consistency was required. I'm rooting for the Edge approach, personally.\n",
